date-released: "2026-09-17"
abstract: >-
  Separates two objects usually conflated in wind-sensitive construction planning: the
  operating limit of an activity, which is a property of the equipment and the operation,
  and the forecast-action trigger, which is a planning rule that depends on forecast
  uncertainty and on the cost of a delayed or cancelled window. Replays archived as-issued
  NOAA GFS surface-gust forecasts against KNMI station observations over 45 stations and
  five years (281,997 decision epochs), with a declared publication-latency rule sampled
  from archive timestamps. The fixed deterministic trigger misses 31-54% of station-defined
  exceedance events; fitting the exceedance probability repairs the Brier skill (+0.27 to
  +0.41 held out) but, paired on identical epochs at matched cost ratios, is not detectably
  different in decision value from an independently tuned raw threshold. Both null results
  replicate in an eleven-month post-sample replay with the model, threshold and limit
  frozen. Costs are model expenses in units of the loss L; no project-level saving is
  measured or claimed.
